// A buffer you can only blank — the type that holds `design/system/0009` D1.
//
// > type syntax is replaced with **spaces**, never deleted, so every line and column of the output
// > matches the input and a stack trace from the result points at the TypeScript that produced it.
// > Newlines inside an erased range survive for the same reason — a multi-line type annotation must
// > not shorten the file.
//
// That is a property of every write in `packages/ts/src/strip.wac`, held by every write being
// careful, and checked at the end by a differential against `ts.transpileModule` over 22 files. It
// is a good check and it is a check: the property is re-established per call site and nothing states
// it.
//
// Here it is the type. `Blanked` starts as a copy of the source and the only mutation is `blank`,
// which cannot change the length and cannot touch a newline. **A stripper that erased a range by
// deleting it would not compile**, because there is no operation for it.

import { Bytes } from "core";

/**
 * The output, which is the input with spans blanked.
 *
 * No constructor from bytes and no `push`. The only way to get one is `Blanked.of(src)`, and the
 * only way to change one is `blank`.
 */
export struct Blanked {
  Bytes src;
  Bytes out;

  Blanked of(Bytes src) { … }

  /**
   * Replace `[from, to)` with spaces, keeping every `\n`.
   *
   * The newline rule is here rather than at each caller, which is the whole of the change: in the
   * shipped file the loop that writes spaces is written once and the *decision* that it must skip
   * newlines is a comment above it. `packages/ts/src/bundle.wac` — 544 lines, and the half of the
   * package this exercise briefly recorded as unwritten — writes into the same buffers and derives
   * the rule again.
   */
  /**
   * Blank a span. **`Bytes`, not `(i32 from, i32 to)`, since 2026-09-05** — and it answers three
   * quarters of the ask below.
   */
  void blank(this, Bytes span) { … }

  /** What came out. Same length as the source, always. */
  Bytes bytes(const this) { return this.out; }
}

// ## What could not be written
//
// **Nothing stops `blank(0, src.len())`.** The type refuses to shorten the file and does not refuse
// to erase the program. That is the correct boundary — a stripper *does* blank arbitrary spans, and
// which spans are types is the whole difficulty — but it is worth being precise about what has been
// bought: the invariant D1 states, and not the one a reader might assume from the name.
//
// **`from` and `to` were two `i32`s and could be crossed — answered, and the residue is the finding.**
// This asked for *a value type where two scalars must agree*, the fourth such request here after
// `@/packages/raster`'s `Rect` and `@/packages/ssz`'s `Gindex` and `Chunk`, and noted that this one
// is the first where the scalars are **offsets into a buffer the receiver also holds**.
//
// `Bytes` answers most of it and was already in `core`. A `Slice<u8>` is `{ of, from, len }` and
// `slice()` traps on a range it does not contain, so `blank(Bytes span)` gets:
//
//   * `from <= to` — guaranteed, a length is not negative;
//   * `to <= of.len()` — guaranteed by construction, because making the slice **is** the check;
//   * and a caller cannot be wrong alone, because it cannot build a bad one.
//
// **What it does not get is that `span.of` is `this.out`.** A slice pairs a position with *an* array;
// it does not pair it with *the* array the receiver means. `blank(otherFile.slice(0, 3))` type-checks
// and this type cannot tell.
//
// That is the honest limit of the whole *carry the subject* programme `../../../QUESTIONS.md` argues
// for, and it took an existing ask to find it. For a fault being rendered the limit does not bite —
// there is one array in play and the slice is a view of it. For a **mutation** it does: the receiver
// has an array of its own, so the question is no longer *where* but *where in what of mine*, and a
// slice answers a different question confidently.
//
// The shape that would close it is a slice whose array is a type parameter — `Slice<u8, Out>` —
// which was filed as the phantom-parameter ask at its eleventh site and is **not** one, for the
// reason this file already gave: the tag would name a *value* rather than a type, and a type
// parameter cannot. `../../../QUESTIONS.md` enumerates the eleven and finds three of them here.
// Nothing on the pages proposes what these three want.
//
// **A `Bytes` that is a copy, per stripper.** `Blanked.of` copies the source so `src` stays readable
// while `out` is written. For a 924-line stripper over a 22-file host that is nothing; for the
// bundler, which concatenates modules, it is a copy per module and the type gives no way to say
// *these two share a source*. The shipped code has the same copy and no type to hang the question
// on, which is why this is a finding rather than a regression.
